+# Sampling strategies: `build_balanced_sample.py` vs. `stratified_subsample.py`
+
+The repo has two scripts for drawing a subset of `first_turns.csv`. They are
+not two versions of the same tool — they use different sampling units and
+solve different problems. This documents both, compares them head-to-head
+on the real dataset, and records a bug that was found and fixed in
+`row_stratified_sample`.
+
+## Dataset structure (context for everything below)
+
+960 rows = 120 hand-crafted base prompts (`original_prompt`) × 4 `use_domain`
+× 2 `use_scenario`/domain. `empathy`/`professionalism` are fully determined
+by `use_domain`; `behavior_category` is fully determined by `cue` (12 cues,
+3 per category); each of the 120 base prompts appears in all 8
+domain/scenario cells exactly once — a complete, unreplicated factorial. Full
+detail and the structural assertions that verify this are in
+`stratified_subsample.py`'s module docstring and `validate_structure()`.
+
+## The three sampling functions
+
+| | `build_balanced_sample.py` (`main`) | `stratified_subsample.py`<br>`block_stratified_sample` | `stratified_subsample.py`<br>`row_stratified_sample` |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Sampling unit | row | **`original_prompt`** | row |
+| Stratifies on | all 6 condition columns (→ 96 domain×scenario×cue cells) | `cue` only | domain × scenario × cue |
+| Size control | fixed N rows/condition (default 2) | prompts kept per cue (default 5/10, or a `frac`) | arbitrary target `n` (default 300) via largest-remainder apportionment |
+| Keeps a selected prompt's full 8-way domain/scenario coverage? | No | **Yes, by construction** | No |
+| Default seed | none — draws a fresh random seed each run unless `--seed` is passed; embeds it in the filename and a `sampling_seed` column | `42` (`--seed` to override) | `42` (`--seed` to override) |
+| Validates dataset structure before running | checks the 6 columns exist | asserts the full nesting/crossing design | (shares `validate_structure()`, run once at the top of `__main__`) |
+| Output | 1 CSV | 1 of 2 CSVs written by `__main__` | 1 of 2 CSVs written by `__main__` |
+
+`build_balanced_sample.py` and `row_stratified_sample` are the same *idea*
+(independent stratified sampling at row level) with different size-control
+math. `block_stratified_sample` is a different idea: treat the base prompt,
+not the row, as what you're sampling, and keep every context replicate of
+whatever you select.
+
+## Why the sampling unit matters: fragmentation
+
+Because `original_prompt` has exactly one row per domain/scenario cell, any
+method that samples rows *independently within each cell* can select a
+prompt's "friendship" variant while dropping its "career development"
+variant — the paper's within-prompt, cross-domain comparison stops being
+possible for that prompt. This was checked directly against the real
+`first_turns.csv`, not just reasoned about:
+
+| Method | Rows | Distinct prompts | Prompts with **all 8** domain/scenario replicates |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `build_balanced_sample.py` (N=2/condition) | 192 | 96 | **0 / 96** |
+| `block_stratified_sample` (k=5/cue) | 480 | 60 | **60 / 60** |
+
+Every prompt `build_balanced_sample.py` touches is partially represented (1–5
+of its 8 replicates); every prompt `block_stratified_sample` touches is fully
+represented. Both methods hit the same condition-column proportions as the
+full dataset (`check_balance()` reports 0.0000 max diff for both on
+`use_domain`, `use_scenario`, `empathy`, `professionalism`, `cue`,
+`behavior_category`) — the difference is entirely about whether a *prompt*
+survives sampling intact, not about whether *conditions* are balanced.
+
+## Bug found and fixed: `row_stratified_sample` seed collapse
+
+`row_stratified_sample` called `df[mask].sample(n=k, random_state=random_state)`
+once per stratum, passing the *same literal* `random_state` every time.
+pandas' `.sample(n=k, random_state=X)` on same-sized groups selects the same
+*relative* row positions regardless of what's in the group — this is
+documented (and independently reproduced) in `build_balanced_sample.py`'s
+header comment, which fixed the identical issue there via
+`group_seed = seed + group_position`. `row_stratified_sample` had not
+received the equivalent fix.
+
+Impact, measured on the real dataset with `n=300` (the default):
+
+| | Before fix | After fix |
+|---|---|---|
+| Distinct `original_prompt` in the sample | 48 / 120 | 112 / 120 |
+| Distinct prompt-selection patterns among the 84 same-sized (k=3) strata | 12 | 81 |
+
+Fix: each stratum now draws with `random_state + position_in_stable_sorted_order`
+instead of the bare `random_state`, mirroring `build_balanced_sample.py`'s
+existing pattern. `block_stratified_sample` was never affected — it draws
+from a single `numpy.random.Generator` whose internal state advances across
+cues, rather than reseeding pandas fresh on every call.
+
+## Which one to use
+
+- **You want the current `--dialogues-per-condition` workflow** (see
+  `docs/guide_dialogues_per_condition_and_seed.md`): use
+  `build_balanced_sample.py`. It's what that flag's condition-grouping in
+  `generator.py` was built against, and its per-run random seed (embedded in
+  the filename and a `sampling_seed` column) is what makes a specific
+  generation run reproducible on demand.
+- **You want a smaller run that stays faithful to the paper's design** — no
+  partially-represented prompts, exact condition proportions, and the
+  ability to compare a model's response to the *same* prompt across domains
+  — use `block_stratified_sample` (the default/recommended path in
+  `stratified_subsample.py`). `k_per_cue` (or `frac`) directly controls how
+  much of the benchmark you run.
+- **You want an arbitrary row count and don't care about prompt
+  fragmentation** (e.g., you only need condition proportions to hold): use
+  `row_stratified_sample`. As of this patch it no longer under-diversifies
+  same-sized strata.
+- **Note:** `stratified_subsample.py`'s outputs are not currently documented
+  as `--custom-prompt-csv` inputs the way `build_balanced_sample.py`'s are,
+  but they retain the same columns `_resolve_condition_groups` in
+  `generator.py` looks for (`use_domain`, `use_scenario`, `empathy`,
+  `professionalism`, `cue`, `category`/`behavior_category`), so they should
+  work as `--custom-prompt-csv` input too — this hasn't been explicitly
+  exercised end-to-end with `--dialogues-per-condition` yet.
+
+## Files changed by this patch
+
+- `stratified_subsample.py` — fixed the seed-reuse bug in
+  `row_stratified_sample`; no change to `block_stratified_sample`,
+  `validate_structure`, `check_balance`, or the CLI.
+- `docs/sampling_strategies_guide.md` — this file.
